  "$schema_notes": "Single source of truth for map-search vocabulary. Sections: numeric_normalization (pre-tokenizer word→digit), object_families (family chips + their aliases), family_chip_label (singular chip labels for relation subjects/objects), candidate_subtypes / sidecar_subtypes (kind → display subtype), candidate_to_poi_kind (CandidateLocation.kind → MapSearchIndexPoiKind for the sidecar), candidate_to_poi_type (CandidateLocation.kind → POI facts.poi_type), road_segment_subtype_by_tag (priority-ordered tag → road-segment subtype, first match wins), road_segment_badges_by_tag (sidecar road_segment_feature tags → extra badge strings), left_turn_phrases_by_tag (protected/unprotected-left tags → search phrases), tag_facts (raw tag → badge strings folded into searchText + exactMapAttributes; the `substring` flag distinguishes exact-match from substring matching), kind_facts (CandidateLocation.kind → implicit facts), fact_value_badges (sidecar fact key → value → badge strings; drives factsToBadges enum renames), overlay_layer_metadata (overlay layer_id → {subtype, family_terms}), operators (relation operator labels + aliases, superset of relation_terms), semantic_terms (alias vocabulary; optional candidate_tags / candidate_kinds anchor the match), relation_terms (parser-level relation operators). Any new search string should land here, not in code. The parser is intentionally narrow: it parses spatial/relational queries against this vocabulary. Polite-framing phrases ('find me', 'where can i find'), intent decomposition, and synonym resolution beyond what's listed here are handled upstream by the LLM tier before it composes a `search_map` query.",
